      "answer": "(0.486, 0.614)",
      "explanation": "This problem requires constructing a confidence interval for a population proportion, p. The sample size is n=400. The number of successes (voters supporting A) is x=220. The sample proportion is p̂ = x/n = 220/400 = 0.55. The formula for a confidence interval for a proportion is p̂ ± Z*_α/₂ * √[p̂(1-p̂)/n]. First, we must check if the sample size is large enough to use the normal approximation. We check if np̂ ≥ 10 and n(1-p̂) ≥ 10. np̂ = 400(0.55) = 220, and n(1-p̂) = 400(0.45) = 180. Both are greater than 10, so the approximation is valid. For a 99% confidence level, the significance level α is 0.01, so α/2 = 0.005. We need the critical Z-value, Z*_0.005, which corresponds to 99.5% (or 0.9950) in the cumulative standard normal distribution. This value is Z*_crit = 2.576. Next, we calculate the standard error of the proportion: SE = √[p̂(1-p̂)/n] = √[(0.55)(0.45)/400] = √[0.2475/400] = √0.00061875 ≈ 0.02487. The margin of error is ME = Z*_crit * SE = 2.576 * 0.02487 ≈ 0.06407. The confidence interval is p̂ ± ME = 0.55 ± 0.06407. The lower bound is 0.55 - 0.06407 = 0.48593. The upper bound is 0.55 + 0.06407 = 0.61407. Rounding to three decimal places, the 99% confidence interval is (0.486, 0.614)."
